<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of the Feathered Serpent</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 200–250</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Temple of Quetzalcoatl, Feathered Serpent Pyramid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Feathered Serpent</a> Pyramid is the third-largest pyramid at Teotihuacan, built around 200 to 250 CE inside the Ciudadela. Its facades bear sculpted heads of feathered serpents alternating with headdress motifs. Excavations found more than a hundred sacrificial victims buried at its foundation, many with hands bound, and a tunnel beneath it held rich offerings. Its facade was later covered by a plain platform.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-feathered-serpent.jpg" alt="Temple of the Feathered Serpent" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wojciech Kocot · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ASb146-Teotihuacan-Templo_Del_Quetzalcoatl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
